## Proof

Let
$$u=\sqrt[3]{\frac{y}{x}},\qquad v=\sqrt[3]{\frac{z}{y}},\qquad w=\sqrt[3]{\frac{x}{z}}.$$
Then $uvw=\sqrt[3]{\tfrac{y}{x}\cdot\tfrac{z}{y}\cdot\tfrac{x}{z}}=1$.

**Recovering $x,y,z$ from $u,v,w$.** From $y=u^{3}x$, $z=v^{3}y=u^{3}v^{3}x$, and $x=w^{3}z=(uvw)^{3}x=x$ (consistent). The constraint $xyz=1$ gives $u^{6}v^{3}x^{3}=1$, hence $x=\dfrac{1}{u^{2}v}=\dfrac{w}{u}$ (using $w=\tfrac{1}{uv}$), and similarly
$$x=\frac{w}{u},\qquad y=\frac{u}{v},\qquad z=\frac{v}{w}.$$

**Reformulating the inequality.** Substituting,
$$(1+x)(1+y)(1+z)=\Bigl(1+\frac{w}{u}\Bigr)\Bigl(1+\frac{u}{v}\Bigr)\Bigl(1+\frac{v}{w}\Bigr),$$
and the right-hand side becomes $2(1+u+v+w)$. So it suffices to prove, for $u,v,w>0$ with $uvw=1$,
$$\Bigl(1+\frac{w}{u}\Bigr)\Bigl(1+\frac{u}{v}\Bigr)\Bigl(1+\frac{v}{w}\Bigr)\ge 2(1+u+v+w).\tag{$\ast$}$$

**Expanding the left-hand side.** Set $A=\tfrac{w}{u},\,B=\tfrac{u}{v},\,C=\tfrac{v}{w}$; note $ABC=1$. Then
$$(1+A)(1+B)(1+C)=2+(A+B+C)+(AB+BC+CA),$$
where
$$A+B+C=\frac{w}{u}+\frac{u}{v}+\frac{v}{w},\qquad AB+BC+CA=\frac{w}{v}+\frac{u}{w}+\frac{v}{u}.$$

Using $uvw=1$ (i.e.\ $\tfrac1u=vw,\ \tfrac1v=uw,\ \tfrac1w=uv$), each ratio becomes a monomial:
$$\frac{w}{u}=vw^{2},\ \frac{u}{v}=u^{2}w,\ \frac{v}{w}=uv^{2},\ \frac{w}{v}=uw^{2},\ \frac{u}{w}=u^{2}v,\ \frac{v}{u}=v^{2}w.$$
Their sum is the full symmetric sum
$$S:=vw^{2}+u^{2}w+uv^{2}+uw^{2}+u^{2}v+v^{2}w=\sum_{\text{sym}}u^{2}v.$$

**A symmetric identity.** Let $p=u+v+w$, $q=uv+vw+wu$, $r=uvw=1$. Expanding,
$$(u+v+w)(uv+vw+wu)=\sum_{\text{sym}}u^{2}v+3uvw=S+3,$$
so $S=pq-3$.

**Reducing ($\ast$).** Inequality ($\ast$) is $2+S\ge 2+2p$, i.e.\ $S\ge 2p$, i.e.\
$$pq-3\ge 2p\iff p(q-2)\ge 3.\tag{$\ast\ast$}$$

**Applying AM–GM.** Since $uvw=1$,
$$p=u+v+w\ge 3\sqrt[3]{uvw}=3,\qquad q=uv+vw+wu\ge 3\sqrt[3]{(uvw)^{2}}=3.$$
Hence $q-2\ge 1>0$, and both factors $p$ and $q-2$ are positive, so
$$p(q-2)\ge 3\cdot 1=3,$$
which is precisely ($\ast\ast$).

**Equality.** Equality in both AM–GM steps requires $u=v=w=1$, i.e.\ $x=y=z=1$, and one checks directly that both sides equal $8$.

Therefore
$$(1+x)(1+y)(1+z)\ge 2\Bigl(1+\sqrt[3]{\tfrac{y}{x}}+\sqrt[3]{\tfrac{z}{y}}+\sqrt[3]{\tfrac{x}{z}}\Bigr)$$
for all positive reals $x,y,z$ with $xyz=1$. $\blacksquare$

